  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer: what political science actually opens up</h2>

  <p>
    A political science degree is not a narrow ticket to one government exam. It is a foundation in
    reading, argument, and understanding how power and institutions actually work — a foundation
    that genuinely feeds nine different career tracks, each with its own timeline, pay structure,
    and risk profile.
  </p>

  <p>
    The confusion is not a shortage of options. It is that almost nobody maps them honestly,
    ranked by which ones actually grow.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-narrow">Why the options feel narrower than they are</h2>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">The standard advice (and why it undersells the degree)</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>"Prepare for UPSC — that's what political science is for."</li>
      <li>"Do a Master's and teach it, if the exam doesn't work out."</li>
      <li>"Maybe journalism, if you're good with words."</li>
      <li>"Or NGO work, if you want to 'do good.'"</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      None of this mentions corporate government affairs, political consulting, or law school —
      three tracks that quietly pay as well as, or better than, the ones everyone already knows
      about.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Part of the problem is prestige order. Families and school counsellors rank options by how
    impressive the title sounds, not by how much growth headroom the work actually has. That
    ordering is exactly backwards for a decision this expensive in time.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tracks">The 9 real tracks, ranked by growth — not by prestige</h2>

  <p>
    The list below is ordered by real scalability and growth headroom, based on how each track
    actually pays as you gain seniority and proof — not by which one sounds most respectable at a
    family gathering. The tracks with the most room to scale, through ownership, corporate ladders,
    or a genuine multiplier credential, come first.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {trackCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{item.badge}</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.name}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
        <div class="feature-card__tags">
          {item.tags.map((tag) => <span class="tag">{tag}</span>)}
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Civil services sits at number 7 on this list, not number 1. That is deliberate. UPSC is
      genuinely prestigious and secure, but its pay is fixed by scale and its ceiling climbs slowly
      over decades. The tracks above it — consulting, corporate government affairs, and law — can
      reach a comparable or higher ceiling faster, for a person willing to build proof rather than
      wait for a rank list.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="consulting">Track 1: political and policy consulting — the scalable-ownership path</h2>

  <p>
    This is the track most political science students never hear about, and it is the one with the
    most genuine room to scale. Dedicated Indian political consultancies — the best known being
    I-PAC, founded by Prashant Kishor — run election-cycle work: voter data analysis, messaging
    strategy, ground-organisation design, and campaign research, hired on a project or retainer
    basis tied to the election calendar.
  </p>

  <p>
    Beyond campaign firms, independent policy consultants advise companies, associations, and
    sometimes government bodies directly on regulatory strategy. A smaller but related version of
    the same idea: building an independent research or political-commentary platform — a policy
    newsletter, a data-driven explainer channel, or an issue-specific consulting practice — that you
    own outright.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Why this scales when the others don't:</strong> you set your own pricing instead of
      sitting on a fixed pay band, demand repeats every election cycle rather than depending on one
      employer's budget, and once you have a track record you can bring on junior researchers
      instead of doing every hour of work yourself. An independent research or commentary platform
      built the same way — your own audience, your own archive of work — carries the identical
      advantage: reach that is not capped by one newsroom's or think tank's payroll.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The entry route is rarely a direct hire straight out of a bachelor's degree. Most people enter
    through a research or data-analysis role at an established consultancy, or by building visible
    independent proof first — a district-level voting-pattern analysis, a policy newsletter with a
    real readership, or a small paid research project for a campaign or client — that shows you can
    do the work before anyone pays you to lead it.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you are seriously weighing the leap into independent consulting rather than staying inside a
    firm, read
    <a href="/blog/freelancing-business/how-to-become-an-independent-consultant-india/">how to become an independent consultant in India</a>
    for the readiness test before you leave a stable paycheck for retainer clients.
  </p>

  <h2 id="corporate-affairs">Track 2: corporate government affairs and public policy</h2>

  <p>
    Every large company operating in a regulated sector — telecom, pharma, fintech, energy, e-commerce
    — now needs someone tracking regulation, managing relationships with ministries and regulators,
    and shaping how the company responds to policy change. That function used to be an afterthought.
    It is now a real, growing corporate team.
  </p>

  <p>
    Current Glassdoor India data puts the average total pay for a Public Policy Manager role at
    roughly ₹29 lakh a year, with a total pay range around ₹30-60 lakh and a median near ₹45 lakh —
    figures that already sit well above most government pay scales at an equivalent career stage.
    Entry-level analyst roles pay considerably less, typically in the ₹6-12 lakh range, and the
    climb to manager level depends on demonstrated regulatory knowledge and relationship-building,
    not tenure alone.
  </p>

  <p>
    This track hires directly from policy research, journalism, and law backgrounds — a political
    science graduate with a strong writing sample and some policy-research exposure is a completely
    normal candidate for an entry government-affairs analyst role, not an outlier.
  </p>

  <h2 id="law">Track 3: law school as a common next step</h2>

  <p>
    Political science and law sit next to each other for a reason — constitutional law, regulatory
    law, and public policy are essentially the same subject matter approached from different
    angles. A three-year LLB after your undergraduate degree, or a CLAT-PG route into an integrated
    postgraduate law programme, is one of the most natural multipliers available on this entire
    list.
  </p>

  <p>
    Law is not one career — litigation, corporate counsel, regulatory and compliance law, and
    policy-adjacent legal research each carry a genuinely different pay ceiling and daily-work
    reality. Rather than re-explain the full CLAT-to-courtroom mechanics here, read
    <a href="/blog/career-options/is-law-a-good-career-in-india/">is law a good career in India</a>
    for the honest breakdown of pay, entry routes, and which lane actually fits which kind of
    person.
  </p>

  <h2 id="policy-research">Track 4: policy research and think tanks</h2>

  <p>
    Research associate and policy analyst roles at institutions such as the Observer Research
    Foundation, Carnegie India, the Centre for Policy Research, PRS Legislative Research, or the
    Takshashila Institution study government decisions, draft briefs, and feed evidence into public
    debate and, sometimes, actual policymaking.
  </p>

  <p>
    Current Payscale India data for Policy Analyst roles shows entry-level pay (under one year)
    averaging around ₹3.4 lakh a year, rising to roughly ₹5 lakh in the one-to-four-year range, with
    an overall average near ₹5.9 lakh. It is not a high-paying entry point on its own — its real
    value is that it is the training ground that feeds directly into Tracks 1, 2, and 9: consulting,
    corporate government affairs, and academia all hire people who can already write a clean policy
    brief.
  </p>

  <p>
    Master's fellowships built specifically for this route — public policy programmes, and
    one-year fellowships that place graduates inside legislators' offices or research
    organisations — are worth researching directly on their own current admission pages rather than
    treated as guaranteed placement pipelines.
  </p>

  <h2 id="journalism">Track 5: journalism and political commentary</h2>

  <p>
    Political reporting, election coverage, and opinion writing are a natural fit for the same
    research-and-argument skills a political science degree builds. The honest catch is that the
    ceiling depends entirely on whether you stay employed or build ownership around the work.
  </p>

  <p>
    Entry-level journalism pay in India commonly runs ₹15,000-25,000 a month, rising to roughly
    ₹50,000-1,00,000 a month for senior reporters and editors with five to ten years of experience —
    a real but modest employed-track ceiling. An independent platform built around the same beat —
    a political newsletter, an explainer channel, or a niche commentary practice — follows the same
    ownership logic as Track 1 above, and can outgrow an employed newsroom salary entirely for
    someone who builds a real audience and reporting credibility.
  </p>

  <p>
    For the fuller comparison of employed journalism against content and commentary work, read
    <a href="/blog/career-options/is-journalism-a-good-career-india/">is journalism a good career in India</a>
    and
    <a href="/blog/career-options/journalism-vs-content-writing-career-india/">journalism vs content writing career India</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="diplomacy">Track 6: international relations and the Foreign Service</h2>

  <p>
    The Indian Foreign Service is recruited through the same UPSC Civil Services Examination as IAS
    and IPS, and places officers at the Ministry of External Affairs and Indian missions overseas.
    It is genuinely prestigious, and genuinely small: the 2025 cycle carried roughly 55 IFS
    vacancies out of 1,087 total civil-services vacancies that year.
  </p>

  <p>
    IFS officers sit on the same central Group A pay scale as IAS and IPS, with allowances that vary
    a great deal by posting country. The career runs through a long ladder of postings — attaché,
    third secretary, second secretary, and upward — and, like the wider civil services, carries a
    second, separate competition described below: getting selected does not guarantee a fast climb
    or a coveted posting.
  </p>

  <p>
    Careers in international relations and development also exist outside the IFS — with the United
    Nations system, multilateral development banks, and international NGOs — but these routes
    generally need a strong postgraduate degree in international relations or public policy plus
    field or research experience, not a bachelor's degree alone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="civil-services">Track 7: civil services — UPSC and State PSC</h2>

  <p>
    This is the option most families reach for first, and the numbers explain both why it is so
    attractive and why it deserves an honest opportunity-cost conversation before you commit years
    to it.
  </p>

