          <p>{item.stage2}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    This is a genuinely different caution from ordinary "hiring is selective" competitiveness that
    shows up in policy research, journalism, or corporate government affairs. In those tracks, one
    strong portfolio can open the door and your growth from there depends mostly on the work you
    keep producing. In civil services and academia, a second, separate filter — postings and
    empanelment on one side, a scarce permanent-post market on the other — sits between "you made
    it" and "you actually reached the pay and influence people picture when they imagine the role."
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">What AI is doing to political science careers</h2>

  <p>
    Every track on this list touches research, writing, and analysis — exactly the layer AI tools
    are changing fastest. The honest picture has a real risk side and a real opportunity side, and
    they are not the same task.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="comparison-col comparison-col--risk">
      <div class="comparison-col__label">Where AI is already automating the work</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Routine, first-pass policy-brief drafting — turning a bill or committee report into a plain-language summary — is already shifting into AI-assisted workflows at many think tanks and corporate-affairs teams.</li>
        <li>Wire-style, fact-based political reporting (rewriting a press release, reporting a routine result) is easier to automate than beat reporting or investigation, so junior "content mill" journalism roles carry more exposure.</li>
        <li>Basic legislative research triage — finding which bills, committees, or notifications touch a specific sector — is now largely a database-search problem AI tools handle faster than a fresher can by hand.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="comparison-col comparison-col--grow">
      <div class="comparison-col__label">Where AI is genuinely creating leverage</div>
      <ul>
        <li>AI-assisted policy research: feeding bills, judgments, and committee reports into an AI tool for a fast first-pass summary, then spending your real time on the judgment call — what it means for a specific client, ministry, or campaign.</li>
        <li>Data-driven political analysis: constituency-level survey data, past voting patterns, and sentiment analysis can now be modelled and visualised far faster, which is exactly why data-literate people are being pulled into political consulting and campaign-strategy roles that used to be pure instinct.</li>
        <li>Independent commentary at scale: one person with real subject knowledge and AI-assisted research and editing can now credibly run a newsletter or explainer channel that used to need a small editorial team — a direct multiplier on Track 1's ownership advantage.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    <strong>Right now:</strong> learn to use AI tools to draft first-pass summaries and structure
    research faster, but keep manually verifying every fact and citation before it goes out under
    your name — a wrong statistic in a published brief or article is a credibility problem, not
    just an embarrassment.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>As adoption matures:</strong> the differentiated value shifts further toward judgment,
    client relationships, and original data work — running your own survey, building your own
    dataset — that a general-purpose AI tool cannot originate on its own. That is a strong argument
    for building data literacy alongside your writing skill now, rather than treating "I read a lot
    and write well" as a complete skill set on its own.
  </p>

