  <h2 id="skills">Skills that matter on every track</h2>

  <p>
    A political science degree gives you a starting point — argument, reading, and institutional
    understanding. What turns that starting point into a real, high-income skill portfolio, and
    eventually earlier financial freedom, is a deliberate stack built on top of it, not the degree
    alone.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Research and argument construction</strong> — the core skill every track above shares. Practise it on real, specific issues, not vague essay-writing.</li>
    <li><strong>Data literacy</strong> — comfort reading survey data, election results, and government datasets. This is the multiplier skill that separates a median policy analyst or consultant from a strong one, and it is directly what AI-assisted political analysis rewards.</li>
    <li><strong>Clear, plain-language writing</strong> — a policy brief, an op-ed, or a client memo is worthless if only other specialists can follow it. This is the proof format most of these tracks actually hire on.</li>
    <li><strong>Negotiation and coalition-building</strong> — the human layer behind consulting, diplomacy, and corporate government affairs that AI does not touch. Client trust and relationship management stay entirely human.</li>
    <li><strong>A working foreign language</strong> — a genuine differentiator for IFS, multilateral, and international-relations-adjacent roles specifically, less critical for the domestic tracks.</li>
    <li><strong>Practical AI fluency</strong> — using AI tools to speed up research and drafting while personally verifying every output, per the AI section above.</li>
  </ul>

