  <h2 id="college">The college and degree decision — an honest take</h2>

  <p>
    Political science is one of the few fields in India where the pattern most students assume —
    "the best option is the most expensive one" — actually runs backwards. Jawaharlal Nehru
    University, Delhi University's top colleges (Hindu College, Miranda House, Lady Shri Ram), and
    Presidency University are simultaneously among the strongest political science departments in
    the country and among the cheapest, because they are public institutions with government-set
    fees.
  </p>

  <p>
    Newer private liberal-arts universities — Ashoka, O.P. Jindal Global University, FLAME,
    Symbiosis — pitch smaller class sizes, global exposure, and stronger placement support, at a
    genuinely different price: often ₹8-15 lakh or more for the full undergraduate programme.
  </p>

  <p>
    Test any spending above the conservative starting benchmark of roughly 10% of your total
    education budget against real, course-specific evidence: does the private option's faculty,
    internship pipeline, and peer network create an advantage you genuinely cannot build yourself
    through a public-college department plus deliberate outside work — think-tank internships,
    published writing, or a strong political-science reading list? If you cannot point to a specific
    advantage, the extra spending is a prestige purchase, not a career investment.
  </p>

  <p>
    Wherever you study, the material that actually builds real analytical skill is inexpensive or
    free: PRS Legislative Research's bill summaries and explainers, the Observer Research
    Foundation and Carnegie India's published research, Economic and Political Weekly, and course
    material from public-policy programmes many universities now publish openly. A student who
    reads this consistently for a year builds a stronger, more current foundation than one who paid
    more for a degree and read none of it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="proof">Building proof before you commit years</h2>

  <p>
    A qualification checklist tells you what is formally required. It cannot tell you whether you
    will actually be good at the work, or enjoy it, before you spend years finding out the hard way.
    Test that first, using <strong>The 3 Gates</strong>.
  </p>

