  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    Do not pick a political science career track from prestige order alone. The label that sounds
    most respectable at a family gathering is not the one with the strongest growth curve, and the
    two-stage competition inside civil services and academia rarely gets explained honestly before
    someone commits years to either.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Pick the track using The 4-Checkpoint Protocol above — your energy, your real runway, current
      market demand, and AI exposure — not just which title sounds most impressive today.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then pass The 3 Gates on one small, real test before you commit an exam attempt, a PhD, or a
      law-entrance season to it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Reaching earlier financial freedom from any of these nine tracks comes down to a genuine
    <strong>high-income skill portfolio</strong> — research, writing, data literacy, and
    negotiation — built deliberately and proven publicly, not the political science label by
    itself. Move toward that with
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career guidance</a>
    if you want a second opinion on your specific situation, or start with the free
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/assessments">career and skill assessments</a>
    if you are still unsure which track genuinely fits you.
  </p>

